Markerless motion capture offers an accessible approach for obtaining such measurements outside of specialized laboratory environments. arly markerless systems shared a common constraint. Without the ability to directly identify anatomical features in an image, they had to work with the data they could extract: the shape of the body itself using techniques such as edge detection @gavrila3DModelbasedTracking1996, optical flow tracking @marzani3DMarkerfreeSystem2001, silhouette extraction @rosenhahnSystemMarkerlessMotion2006, and 3D visual hull reconstruction @corazzaMarkerlessMotionCapture2006. While these methods could estimate body position in space, they were complex, often computationally intensive methods that required significant expertise to implement. 

A fundamental shift towards modern markerless motion capture occurred with the introduction of deep learning-based pose estimation. 
The release of DeepPose @toshevDeepPoseHumanPose2014 in 2014 and OpenPose@caoOpenPoseRealtimeMultiPerson2019 in 2017 marked a transition point, giving researchers access to methods that could learn to infer joint locations directly. With this, deep-learning methods became state-of-the-art and the dominant approach to markerless motion capture @nagornyComprehensiveReviewRealTime2025. 

Pose estimation has become a fundamental part of markerless motion capture. However, different pose estimation sfotware have different strengths and weaknesses, and the performance of a given pose estimation model can vary across different tasks and populations. For example, models trained on able-bodied individuals may struggle to track individuals with altered limb morphology, such as limb amputations @zhouDiffusionModelsEnable2025 @cimorelliPortableInclinicVideobased2022. As such, there is a need for markerless motion capture systems that can support interchangeable pose estimation backends, allowing researchers to select or compare algorithms that might be better suited to specific populations or research needs.





It is very easy for us to overlook the complexities of a simple action such as standing up. And yet the act of rising from a chair utilizes dozens of muscles and joints across the entire body, all working to execute a series of complex movements that shift the body's center of mass from a stable seated position into less stable standing posture - smoothly, quickly and without falling over @liKinematicAnalysisHuman2021. Even an action this mundane can tell us a great deal. The time it takes someone to stand up from a chair five times is, in fact, a validated clinical assessment of functional health @FiveTimesSit2013. This pattern is true of a great deal of movements - walking, balancing, reaching - embedded within these movements is information that provides a window into the inner workings of the body. 

Quantitative motion analysis is, then, the process of turning movement into measurement and extracting meaningful parameters from it.  Marker-based motion capture remains the gold-standard for capturing accurate kinematic data, but is generally unsuitable for widespread clinical use (see Chapter 1). 

Markerless motion capture offers an accessible approach for obtaining such measurements outside of specialized laboratory environments. However, existing systems may be limited by cost, hardware requirements or methodological constraints (see Chapter 2). 

